Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.880 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined today by cari  Blake injure who's a criminal justice  reporter for the Houston Chronicle she  spent two years in correctional  facilities and now exposes problems  within them something we talk about  often on the program Kari thanks for  talking to me really appreciate it  thanks for having me so let's start with  some of the work that you do now when  you talk about exposing problems within  the criminal justice system what is your  perspective as to what the primary  problems are as someone who has been  within the system because from outside  we could focus on nonviolent  incarceration we could talk about the  way inmates are treated we could talk  about the school to Prison Pipeline  private prisons there are so many  different directions we could go what's  your perspective given your relationship  to the system well I mean I think that's  a question with a lot of answers in  terms of what the problems are I mean  there's a lot of problems with indigent  defense with you know prosecutions with  wake or to run I mean I focused a lot on  the prison system because that was  something that in Texas was really not  being covered in any substantive way I  think and I think that's partly because  the Texas prison system is so spread out  it's you know 104 prisons across the  state it's sort of not local news to any  one particular paper because they're all  over so it you know I would hesitate to  say that this is sort of one of the  biggest problems in criminal justice or  anything but it is something that was  not being covered so I've delved into  conditions and you know medical care for  inmates and some aspects of Prisons that  just weren't being covered can you talk  a little bit about your life story and  how you ended up serving two years and  then maybe we can talk about what you  experienced firsthand yeah sure so I was  a heroin addict off and on for nine  years and then when I was in school I  was trying to finish up college despite  you know despite daily drug use and I  ended up getting arrested with a few  days left before I was supposed to  graduate and I went to prison for two  years and you know so I mean I  out and ended up getting my life  together and becoming a reporter and  it's you know it not the most common  outcome from incarceration and I was  incredibly lucky they you know afforded  this opportunity to rebuild my life yeah  what was different I mean as you're  alluding to and as we've talked about  the transition out is an extraordinarily  complex and sort of fraught with  difficulty situation an under-resourced  circumstance for sure what was different  about your transition out that allowed  you to do what you're doing now well I  mean there's a lot of things that were  different I mean for one I came in with  an education almost completed so when I  came out I had you know like two classes  left to graduate and then I had a degree  which I mean that enough itself is is  huge I also had support a family I had a  place to say you know I think they you  know white privilege plays a role I  think that uh you know class privilege  play plays a role and it was still hard  I mean I had all these advantages and  it's still hard to rebuild your life  with a felony regardless but I was very  fortunate during your time during those  two years talk a little bit about the  conditions you experienced not that  they're exactly representative of what  everybody experiences of course and talk  a little bit about maybe the solitary  confinement experience as well right so  yeah conditions do vary a lot from one  prison system to the next but there are  certain things that I think are somewhat  consistent you know medical care is  problematic in a lot of states and you  know and then for me the use of solitary  confinement was was really difficult I  was in solitary maybe three different  times for just a few days each and I did  not handle it well one of them was just  like routine medical isolation and then  one was because I'd been transferred and  one time was punitive and you know I I  just I remember the first time I  went in there and you know they'd close  the door behind me in it with this like  neon why like maddeningly neon white  cell and you know you have no clock it's  freezing in there you have like nothing  to do and you know I I just I just lost  it like I a very quickly lost track of  sort of time and like when I was awaking  when I was it was I was it was like  being buried alive I think I've said  that before  and that's really sort of the best  descriptor I think a lot of people sort  of think of solitary confinement as like  oh I like spending time alone in  solitary wouldn't be that bad but you  know I like spending time alone it's not  like that at all yeah I mean it seems  that no matter what the emotional state  of someone before that that there is no  way to justify that as part of a system  that's meant to rehabilitate I mean I  just don't see any way that that that  would be something that you could  justify with any kind of in any kind of  serious way well I mean I don't think  there's much way to claim that it's that  it helps from a rehabilitative  standpoint I mean typically the  justification that that prisons would  use is that it's about the safety and  security of the facility yeah but you  know I mean that that varies there's  different you know there's different  reasons that systems put people in  solitary confinement  and you know which which means that  there's sort of different issues to  address in terms of how it's being used  and how it should or shouldn't be used  so maybe we can start with like what you  would consider maybe some of the lowest  hanging fruit based on your experience  and the day to day problems that you had  what are the the sort of easiest  problems that could be fixed well I  think I think that varies a lot from one  prison system to the next you know I  think in you know I think in in Texas  one of the things that I'm seeing is  that you know a lot of the problems that  I'm hearing about here sort of stem from  from understand  laughing you know things like prison  violence and you know that there's been  a lot of pervasive issues with like drug  smuggling and you know it seemed and and  you know lack of medical treatment which  sometimes it's just because there aren't  enough staff to handle in getting people  to medical appointments and you know I  think it's probably unexpected for you  know a former inmate to be saying well  you need more guards but that does seem  to be one of the problems that we're  seeing here in Texas and part of that's  because they're not certified peace  officers so they don't get they don't  get very good pay compared to other  states or compared to you know other  state government jobs even so it makes  it hard to retain them but you know New  York didn't have that problem so I mean  this is you know they're much better  staffed like in terms of officer to  inmate ratio and you know I think I  think there are two say oh this is one  likes like this is the biggest problem I  think there was some specific prisons  that had extremely problematic cultures  there and I've written about some of  them and I've experienced some of them  and you know another thing that I think  part of that relates to is about the  placement of these humans and that  something is true in many states is that  a lot of the prisons are in extremely  rural areas and I think that can be  problematic for a number of reasons  including that you can end up in a  situation where the you know the you end  up with a bunch of you know old white  men guarding a bunch of young minority  inmates from urban areas and I think  that that has in some situations posed  its own problems and you know then you  end up with the inmates being further  from home so to sum that up I think that  the location of Prisons is problematic  and I think that the understaffing  issues have been consistent concern and  that's not even touching on like the  specifics of unaddressed medical issues  or you know civil rights issues which  are you know a whole other can of worms  if we widen our  scope a little bit beyond prisons to the  criminal justice system and that's also  something I mean you're covering prisons  you're also covering the criminal  justice system of course what do you  think the priorities should be in terms  of our elected officials when it comes  to the criminal justice system and this  is I mean it's wide open right war on  drugs plea deal industrial complex take  it any any way you want  well I mean I mean as a reporter I can't  really weigh in on you know what policy  changes I think should occur you know I  can as in you know the case of what I  just said prisons I can sort of weigh in  on what I see that keeps coming up as an  issue but I you know I have to maintain  neutrality here I can't start  recommending policy changes can you talk  a little bit let me see how I can  reframe it if we look at the current  Justice Department have you seen  anything take place that is any kind of  significant shift in policy and whether  you want to assign good or bad to it  that's your call but what what are the  sort of key changes we've seen in terms  of criminal justice under the current  Justice Department well I think it's not  just the current Justice Department but  I mean obviously everyone is seeing this  you know long term shift away from the  tough on crime attitudes of the 90s and  you know I mean that's been you know as  someone who was like directly impacted  by the you know war on drugs you know  that that's been interesting to see in  New York in particular and again this  has not been under the current Justice  Department because this would be about  state law you know they rolled back the  Rockefeller laws which were some of the  most notoriously punitive mandatory  minimum sentencing laws in the country  and they rolled those back progressively  over a number of years and I very  narrowly avoided being sentenced under  those because I got arrested about a  year after the last parts of them were  were taken off the books and you know I  could have been doing 15 to life had I'd  been sentenced under the old law so and  you know now with you know now we're  seeing like federal legislation that  would you know do things  reduce the crack cocaine the crack  powder sentencing disparity so I mean I  think we've seen this happen in various  states and you know we're seeing some  you know change on the federal level and  you know yeah it's happening some of  it's happening under this administration  but this is part of a longer trajectory  stepping away from a tough on crime era  can you talk a little bit about in your  conversations with other people who were  in the system when you were in the  system  how much discussion was there about this  type of stuff about policy about  sentencing about the types of things  that you're you're doing a lot of  coverage of I don't part of its because  of when I was in I I suspect that might  be different now like I was doing my  time like 2011-2012 you got to figure  you know it takes a couple years for the  major discussions on the outside to sort  of percolate through the prison's so I  suspect that now now things like the war  on drugs or stepping back from the war  on drugs might be getting you know more  discussion in prison than they were when  I was there but you know also to an  extent like you know you're in you're  already there like you're already stuck  in prison like I think for a lot of  people there's some sense of like how  much do we want to talk about how  terrible terrible this office you know I  mean that's kind of what it felt like  you know we talked about conditions and  you know there's a lot of sort of  dreaming about various lawsuits people  would like to see happen but there's not  a lot of discussion about policy change  Kenny Blankenship is doing excellent  work for the Houston Chronicle as a  criminal justice reporter Kerry I really  appreciate you talking to us today  thanks for having me

